ce AI coding agent</span>\n   534→                            <span class=\"tag\">GitHub Trending</span>\n   535→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 12</span>\n   536→                        </div>\n   537→                    </div>\n   538→\n   539→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   540→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   541→                            <span class=\"upvote\">▲</span>\n   542→                            <a href=\"https://github.com/millionco/react-doctor\" target=\"_blank\">react-doctor</a>\n   543→                        </div>\n   544→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   545→                            Your agent writes bad React. This catches it - a specialized linter for detecting and fixing AI-generated React code quality issues.\n   546→                        </div>\n   547→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   548→                            <span class=\"tag\">workflow-specific AI tool</span>\n   549→                            <span class=\"tag\">GitHub Trending</span>\n   550→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 12</span>\n   551→                        </div>\n   552→                    </div>\n   553→\n   554→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   555→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   556→                            <span class=\"upvote\">▲</span>\n   557→                            <a href=\"https://github.com/salmanjavaid/rapunzel\" target=\"_blank\">Rapunzel</a>\n   558→                        </div>\n   559→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   560→                            A tree-style tab terminal emulator designed for Codex, Claude, and Gemini AI coding assistants. Helps manage multiple terminal sessions when working with AI coding CLIs.\n   561→                        </div>\n   562→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   563→                            <span class=\"tag\">AI coding CLI</span>\n   564→                            <span class=\"tag\">Show HN</span>\n   565→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 11</span>\n   566→                        </div>\n   567→                    </div>\n   568→\n   569→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   570→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   571→                            <span class=\"upvote\">▲</span>\n   572→                            <a href=\"https://producthunt.com/posts/graphbit-prflow\" target=\"_blank\">Graphbit PRFlow - AI Code Review Agent</a>\n   573→                        </div>\n   574→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   575→                            AI code review agent that automates PR reviews with workflow orchestration. Integrates into development workflows to catch bugs before merge.\n   576→                        </div>\n   577→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   578→                            <span class=\"tag\">code agent</span>\n   579→                            <span class=\"tag\">Product Hunt</span>\n   580→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 12</span>\n   581→                        </div>\n   582→                    </div>\n   583→\n   584→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   585→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   586→                            <span class=\"upvote\">▲</span>\n   587→                            <a href=\"https://producthunt.com/posts/kilo-code\" target=\"_blank\">Kilo Code v7 for VS Code</a>\n   588→                        </div>\n   589→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   590→                            AI coding assistant deeply integrated into VS Code. Provides AI-powered code completion, refactoring, and workflow assistance within the existing IDE.\n   591→                        </div>\n   592→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   593→                            <span class=\"tag\">IDE-integrated AI tools</span>\n   594→                            <span class=\"tag\">Product Hunt</span>\n   595→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 12</span>\n   596→                        </div>\n   597→                    </div>\n   598→\n   599→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   600→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   601→                            <span class=\"upvote\">▲</span>\n   602→                            <a href=\"https://producthunt.com/posts/cachetray\" target=\"_blank\">CacheTray</a>\n   603→                        </div>\n   604→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   605→                            Capture anything on your screen and send it to Claude or ChatGPT in one click. Workflow agent that helps developers quickly get AI assistance on code snippets, errors, or documentation.\n   606→                        </div>\n   607→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   608→                            <span class=\"tag\">workflow agent</span>\n   609→                            <span class=\"tag\">Product Hunt</span>\n   610→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 12</span>\n   611→                        </div>\n   612→                    </div>\n   613→\n   614→                    <div class=\"item\">\n   615→                        <div class=\"item-title\">\n   616→                            <span class=\"upvote\">▲</span>\n   617→                            <a href=\"https://mintlify.com\" target=\"_blank\">Mintlify</a>\n   618→                        </div>\n   619→                        <div class=\"item-description\">\n   620→                            Knowledge infrastructure for AI agents. Helps AI agents understand and navigate codebases by providing structured documentation and context about projects.\n   621→                        </div>\n   622→                        <div class=\"item-meta\">\n   623→                            <span class=\"tag\">workflow agent</span>\n   624→                            <span class=\"tag\">Product Hunt</span>\n   625→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 12</span>\n   626→                        </div>\n   627→                    </div>\n   628→                </section>\n   629→\n   630→                <!-- Whitelist Updates Section -->\n   631→                <section id=\"updates\">\n   632→                    <div class=\"section-header\">Whitelist Updates</div>\n   633→\n   634→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   635→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Claude Code</div>\n   636→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   637→                            v2.1.138: Internal fixes\n   638→                        </div>\n   639→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   640→                            Minor version with internal stability improvements and fixes.\n   641→                        </div>\n   642→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   643→                            <span class=\"update-type release\">release</span>\n   644→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 8, 2026</span>\n   645→                        </div>\n   646→                    </div>\n   647→\n   648→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   649→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Claude Code</div>\n   650→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   651→                            v2.1.137: VSCode extension Windows fix\n   652→                        </div>\n   653→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   654→                            Fixed VSCode extension failing to activate on Windows systems.\n   655→                        </div>\n   656→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   657→                            <span class=\"update-type bugfix\">bugfix</span>\n   658→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 7, 2026</span>\n   659→                        </div>\n   660→                    </div>\n   661→\n   662→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   663→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Claude Code</div>\n   664→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   665→                            v2.1.136: Enterprise feedback surveys, Auto Mode hard deny rules\n   666→                        </div>\n   667→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   668→                            Added enterprise feedback survey support via OpenTelemetry, auto mode hard deny classifier rules that unconditionally block actions, fixed MCP servers/plugins disappearing after /clear, OAuth refresh token issues for remote MCP servers, login loops, API errors with extended thinking, and over 30 additional bug fixes.\n   669→                        </div>\n   670→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   671→                            <span class=\"update-type feature\">feature</span>\n   672→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 6, 2026</span>\n   673→                        </div>\n   674→                    </div>\n   675→\n   676→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   677→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Claude Code</div>\n   678→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   679→                            Week 19: Plugins from .zip archives and URLs\n   680→                        </div>\n   681→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   682→                            Claude Code v2.1.128–v2.1.136 introduced plugin loading from .zip archives and URLs via --plugin-url flag, worktree.baseRef setting to choose branching source, auto mode hard deny rules, and hooks now expose effort level via effort.level and $CLAUDE_EFFORT environment variable.\n   683→                        </div>\n   684→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   685→                            <span class=\"update-type feature\">feature</span>\n   686→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 4, 2026</span>\n   687→                        </div>\n   688→                    </div>\n   689→\n   690→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   691→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Cursor</div>\n   692→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   693→                            Bugbot Effort Levels and PR Review enhancements\n   694→                        </div>\n   695→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   696→                            Teams admins and individual users can now customize Bugbot's effort level for PR reviews with three configurations: Default (optimized for speed), High (more reasoning, more expensive), or Custom (natural language instructions). Also introduced new PR review experience with inline review threads, commits tab, and changes tab with file tree navigation.\n   697→                        </div>\n   698→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   699→                            <span class=\"update-type feature\">feature</span>\n   700→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 11, 2026</span>\n   701→                        </div>\n   702→                    </div>\n   703→\n   704→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   705→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Cursor</div>\n   706→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   707→                            v3.3: Cloud agent reliability fixes\n   708→                        </div>\n   709→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   710→                            Fixed various cloud agent timing and hydration edge cases that could degrade reliability.\n   711→                        </div>\n   712→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   713→                            <span class=\"update-type bugfix\">bugfix</span>\n   714→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 6, 2026</span>\n   715→                        </div>\n   716→                    </div>\n   717→\n   718→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   719→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Cursor</div>\n   720→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   721→                            Microsoft Teams integration\n   722→                        </div>\n   723→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   724→                            Cursor is now available in Microsoft Teams. Users can mention @Cursor in any Teams channel to delegate tasks to cloud agents or pull information from Cursor. It automatically picks the right repository and model based on prompt and recent agent activity.\n   725→                        </div>\n   726→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   727→                            <span class=\"update-type feature\">feature</span>\n   728→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">May 1, 2026</span>\n   729→                        </div>\n   730→                    </div>\n   731→\n   732→                    <div class=\"update-item\">\n   733→                        <div class=\"update-product\">Cursor</div>\n   734→                        <div class=\"update-title\">\n   735→                            PR Review, Build Plan in Parallel, and Split PRs\n   736→                        </div>\n   737→                        <div class=\"update-description\">\n   738→                            New PR review experience with Reviews tab showing inline threads and top-level comments, Commits tab for focused history, and Changes tab with file tree. Can now execute on plans faster by multitasking across tasks with 'Build in Parallel' feature, and split changes into PRs using quick action that identifies logical slices.\n   739→                        </div>\n   740→                        <div class=\"update-meta\">\n   741→                            <span class=\"update-type feature\">feature</span>\n   742→                            <span class=\"timestamp\">Apr 27, 2026</span>\n   743→                        </div>\n   744→                    </div>\n   745→                </section>\n   746→\n   747→                <!-- Technical Insights Section -->\n   748→                <section id=\"insights\">\n   749→                    <div class=\"section-header\">Technical Insights</div>\n   750→\n   751→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   752→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   753→                            <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40811543\" target=\"_blank\">Software engineering may no longer be a lifetime career</a>\n   754→                        </div>\n   755→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">seangoedecke.com</div>\n   756→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   757→                            Thought-provoking discussion about the sustainability of software engineering as a long-term career in the age of AI.\n   758→                        </div>\n   759→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">HN</span>\n   760→                    </div>\n   761→\n   762→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   763→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   764→                            <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40811987\" target=\"_blank\">Local AI needs to be the norm</a>\n   765→                        </div>\n   766→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">unix.foo</div>\n   767→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   768→                            Highly discussed post (1669 points) advocating for local AI deployment. Explores privacy, cost, and control benefits of running AI models locally.\n   769→                        </div>\n   770→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">HN</span>\n   771→                    </div>\n   772→\n   773→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   774→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   775→                            <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/5/our-ai-started-a-cafe-in-stockholm/\" target=\"_blank\">Our AI started a cafe in Stockholm</a>\n   776→                        </div>\n   777→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Simon Willison</div>\n   778→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   779→                            Andon Labs ran an AI-run cafe experiment in Stockholm, Sweden. Raises ethical concerns when AI managers waste time of humans who haven't opted into the experiment.\n   780→                        </div>\n   781→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">Blog</span>\n   782→                    </div>\n   783→\n   784→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   785→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   786→                            <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/7/behind-the-scenes-hardening-firefox-with-claude-mythos-preview/\" target=\"_blank\">Behind the Scenes Hardening Firefox with Claude Mythos Preview</a>\n   787→                        </div>\n   788→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Simon Willison</div>\n   789→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   790→                            Mozilla used access to Claude Mythos preview to locate and fix hundreds of vulnerabilities in Firefox. They went from fixing 20-30 security bugs per month to 423 in April 2026.\n   791→                        </div>\n   792→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">Blog</span>\n   793→                    </div>\n   794→\n   795→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   796→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   797→                            <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/8/using-claude-code-the-unreasonable-effectiveness-of-html/\" target=\"_blank\">Using Claude Code: The Unreasonable Effectiveness of HTML</a>\n   798→                        </div>\n   799→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Simon Willison</div>\n   800→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   801→                            Thariq Shihipar advocates for HTML over Markdown as an output format for Claude. HTML enables SVG diagrams, interactive widgets, in-page navigation, and richer explanations.\n   802→                        </div>\n   803→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">Blog</span>\n   804→                    </div>\n   805→\n   806→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   807→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   808→                            <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/6/vibe-coding-and-agentic-engineering/\" target=\"_blank\">Vibe coding and agentic engineering are getting closer</a>\n   809→                        </div>\n   810→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Simon Willison</div>\n   811→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   812→                            Simon discusses his disturbing realization that vibe coding and agentic engineering have started to converge in his own work.\n   813→                        </div>\n   814→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">Blog</span>\n   815→                    </div>\n   816→\n   817→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   818→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   819→                            <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40812345\" target=\"_blank\">I'm going back to writing code by hand</a>\n   820→                        </div>\n   821→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">k10s.dev</div>\n   822→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   823→                            Popular discussion (814 points) about developers choosing to return to manual coding despite advances in AI coding tools.\n   824→                        </div>\n   825→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">HN</span>\n   826→                    </div>\n   827→\n   828→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   829→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   830→                            <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40813210\" target=\"_blank\">Mythos Finds a Curl Vulnerability</a>\n   831→                        </div>\n   832→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">haxx.se</div>\n   833→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   834→                            Claude Mythos discovered a vulnerability in curl, highlighting the growing capability of AI systems in security research.\n   835→                        </div>\n   836→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">HN</span>\n   837→                    </div>\n   838→\n   839→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   840→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   841→                            <a href=\"https://changelog.com/podcast/587\" target=\"_blank\">Intent, Amelia Wattenberger, and the agent-first redesign</a>\n   842→                        </div>\n   843→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">The Changelog</div>\n   844→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   845→                            Amelia Wattenberger discusses designing Intent at Augment Code in an agent-first world. Covers the identity crisis developers face as agents take over the keyboard.\n   846→                        </div>\n   847→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">Podcast</span>\n   848→                    </div>\n   849→\n   850→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   851→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   852→                            <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/08/how-anthropics-mythos-firefox-cybersecurity/\" target=\"_blank\">How Anthropic's Mythos has rewritten Firefox's approach to cybersecurity</a>\n   853→                        </div>\n   854→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Russell Brandom</div>\n   855→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   856→                            TechCrunch covers how Mozilla used Claude Mythos to dramatically scale up vulnerability discovery in Firefox, going from 20-30 bugs per month to 423 in April.\n   857→                        </div>\n   858→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">TechCrunch</span>\n   859→                    </div>\n   860→\n   861→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   862→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   863→                            <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40811876\" target=\"_blank\">An AI coding agent needs to reduce your maintenance costs</a>\n   864→                        </div>\n   865→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">jamesshore.com</div>\n   866→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   867→                            James Shore explores the economic reality of AI coding agents - they should reduce maintenance costs, not just initial development time.\n   868→                        </div>\n   869→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">HN</span>\n   870→                    </div>\n   871→\n   872→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   873→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   874→                            <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/10/nyt-ai-hallucination-correction/\" target=\"_blank\">New York Times AI Hallucination Correction</a>\n   875→                        </div>\n   876→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Simon Willison</div>\n   877→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   878→                            The New York Times issued an editors' note after learning that a remark attributed to Pierre Poilievre was actually an AI-generated summary rendered as a quotation.\n   879→                        </div>\n   880→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">Blog</span>\n   881→                    </div>\n   882→\n   883→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   884→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   885→                            <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=40812754\" target=\"_blank\">Running local models on an M4 with 24GB memory</a>\n   886→                        </div>\n   887→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">jola.dev</div>\n   888→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   889→                            Practical guide and discussion about running local LLMs on Apple's M4 hardware with 24GB RAM.\n   890→                        </div>\n   891→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">HN</span>\n   892→                    </div>\n   893→\n   894→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   895→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   896→                            <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/05/07/cloudflare-ai-jobs-obsolete-revenue-record/\" target=\"_blank\">Cloudflare says AI made 1,100 jobs obsolete</a>\n   897→                        </div>\n   898→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Julie Bort</div>\n   899→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   900→                            Cloudflare revealed that AI automation eliminated 1,100 positions while the company achieved record revenue.\n   901→                        </div>\n   902→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">TechCrunch</span>\n   903→                    </div>\n   904→\n   905→                    <div class=\"insight-item\">\n   906→                        <div class=\"insight-title\">\n   907→                            <a href=\"https://simonwillison.net/2026/May/9/openais-webrtc-problem/\" target=\"_blank\">OpenAI's WebRTC Problem: Low Latency vs Prompt Accuracy</a>\n   908→                        </div>\n   909→                        <div class=\"insight-author\">Simon Willison</div>\n   910→                        <div class=\"insight-description\">\n   911→                            Luke Curley discusses how WebRTC degrades and drops audio packets during poor network conditions - problematic for LLM voice interactions where accuracy matters.\n   912→                        </div>\n   913→                        <span class=\"insight-source\">Blog</span>\n   914→                    </div>\n   915→                </section>\n   916→            </div>\n   917→        </div>\n   918→    </main>\n   919→\n   920→    <footer id=\"about\">\n   921→        <div class=\"container\">\n   922→            <div class=\"footer-links\">\n   923→                <a href=\"#\">Guidelines</a>\n   924→                <a href=\"#\">FAQ</a>\n   925→                <a href=\"#\">API</a>\n   926→                <a href=\"#\">Legal</a>\n   927→                <a href=\"#\">Contact</a>\n   928→            </div>\n   929→            <div class=\"footer-text\">\n   930→                Agentic Coding Research Report - Daily digest of AI coding developments\n   931→            </div>\n   932→        </div>\n   933→    </footer>\n   934→</body>\n   935→</html>\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"
      }
    ]
  },
